---
// <BcnTrackingFeed> — the four views of the Obligation Tracking page.
//
// WHAT REPLACED THE INBOX'S PIVOT. The Obligations Inbox had Open/Seen and a filing verb on
// every notice. Both are gone. Nothing is owed to a feed, and a standing duty is still on
// tomorrow, so marking it seen never meant anything — the only completable thing is a
// to-do, and a to-do is an Action, which has a real status model of its own. In their place
// are four views that answer questions with actual answers:
//
//   All        a timeline of events, each carrying the duties it switched on
//   Important  the reader's own slice of what is in force — per-user, a saved filter
//   To-do      notices that have fired
//   Pinned     the duties this reader is watching, and what has reached them
//
// PINNED REPLACED ONGOING ON 2026-09-17, and the swap is the point rather than a rename.
// Ongoing was the inventory — "what is on right now" — and to exist at all it had to AUTHOR
// which activities were under way, because nothing in the fixture records that an obligation is
// currently in force. Pinning does not close that gap; it stops pretending to. The reader says
// what they are watching, which is a claim the software can actually keep.
//
// WHAT THE PAGE GAVE UP: it no longer answers "what is in force right now" for anybody. That is
// a deliberate subtraction, and it should be reversed the moment obligations carry in-effect
// conditions. All four views are now cuts of ONE event set — everything, my subject areas, what
// is owed, and what I pinned — which Ongoing never was.
//
// EVERY VIEW IS TWO PANES: the timeline (or list) on the LEFT, and the obligations related
// to whatever is selected on the RIGHT. That geometry is the part of the inbox that was
// always right, and it is why the page can drop the threading language without losing the
// idea behind it — "why am I seeing this" stays structural. bcn-tracking-workspace owns the
// frame; this component owns which view is showing and what a parent means in each.
//
// ALL GROUPS BY EVENT, NEVER BY DUTY. One sighting switches on several duties, so a flat
// list would repeat the same sighting once per duty. Prod already returns this shape — its
// observation service hands back an observation together with the commitments it triggers,
// described in its own code as a parent with children.
//
// NO SEVERITY ORDERING anywhere. It appears nowhere in the source documents; we invented it
// and dropped it. A timeline sorts by time, and the things needing action have their own
// view. To-do sorts on whether the permit states a strict deadline, which is registry data.
//
// EVERY PANEL RENDERS AT BUILD TIME — the rule inherited from the evidence inbox. Astro is
// compile-time; a panel assembled from a template literal bypasses the design system and no
// gate can see it. esa-tab-layout only reveals a panel that is already there.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: esa-tab-layout IS the view switcher (the canonical ARIA tablist, with
// roving keyboard nav already in it), esa-alert-box the provenance note, and
// bcn-tracking-workspace the two-pane frame inside each view. Checked Ecology for a
// feed/timeline lego (none) and Beacon (its activity lists are Angular table components).
import EsaAlertBox from '@esa/ecology/esa-alert-box.astro';
import BcnTrackingWorkspace from './BcnTrackingWorkspace.astro';
import { PINNED, TODO_SHORTFALL, TRACKING_TOTALS, VIEWS } from '../../data/obligation-tracking';

// NO COUNTS ON THE TABS. They were the loudest thing on the page for the least useful fact,
// and once the volumes became realistic (All 7, To-do 3) the numbers stopped carrying anything
// a reader needed before clicking. The rail below shows what is there. (Kim, 2026-09-16.)
const TABS = VIEWS.map((v) => ({ label: v.label }));

// What a PARENT is in each view — the rail's heading, so the left column always says what
// it is a list of rather than leaving the reader to infer it.
//
// ONGOING'S PARENT IS A REASON, not an event: each rail row is the condition that puts its
// duties in force. The key was missing until 2026-09-17, which rendered the fourth rail with an
// EMPTY heading and left its listbox with no accessible name — esbuild strips types, so nothing
// failed. Pinned's by-event rail is named here; its by-obligation rail — the native one — is
// named in RAIL_BY_OBLIGATION beside the others.
const RAIL = {
  all: 'Events',
  important: 'In your filter',
  todo: 'Events that owe a notice',
  pinned: 'Events touching your pins',
} as const;
---

<section class="bcn-tf">
  <esa-tab-layout tabs={JSON.stringify(TABS)} appearance="underline" size="md" active-index="0">
    <div slot="panel-0">
      <BcnTrackingWorkspace view="all" railLabel={RAIL.all} />
    </div>

    <div slot="panel-1">
      <BcnTrackingWorkspace view="important" railLabel={RAIL.important} />
    </div>

    <div slot="panel-2" class="stack" data-gap="md">
      <BcnTrackingWorkspace view="todo" railLabel={RAIL.todo} />
      <EsaAlertBox variant="info" title="Notify only">
        A duty reaches this view when an event leaves somebody owing a notice — class Notify, and
        only Notify. Monitor is a standing cadence — something that is ON rather than owed, which is
        why it reaches you by being pinned rather than by appearing here. Adhere is a rule you
        comply with, and Roster is a standing qualification.
        This permit drafted {TODO_SHORTFALL.notifyInFixture} Notify duties out of
        {TODO_SHORTFALL.total}, against {TODO_SHORTFALL.monitorInFixture} Monitor — which is why
        this view is short. The hand-built registry of the same permits had 50 notices; nobody has
        yet checked whether the drafting pass routes them to Actions instead.
      </EsaAlertBox>
    </div>

    <div slot="panel-3" class="stack" data-gap="md">
      <BcnTrackingWorkspace view="pinned" railLabel={RAIL.pinned} />
      <EsaAlertBox variant="info" title="Pins are authored">
        Beacon has no pin table, so these {PINNED.length} are a stand-in for a reader's own
        selection. What each pin then shows is real: the events that reached it are the same matches
        the other views run. This view replaced Ongoing, which listed what was in force by authoring
        which activities were under way — none of the {TRACKING_TOTALS.obligations} obligations
        records that it is currently live, so the page no longer claims to know.
      </EsaAlertBox>
    </div>

  </esa-tab-layout>
</section>

<script>
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-tab-layout';
</script>

{/*
  NO CSS. The tab-badge muting that used to sit here (--_tab-badge-bg / --_tab-badge-color)
  went with the counts: TABS carries labels only, so there is no badge left to quiet. If a
  count ever returns, the lego exposes both as custom properties — muting it is configuration,
  not an override reaching inside it. Removed 2026-09-17.
*/}
